  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1131 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nShower, thunderstorm potential Friday morning\n\nEarly Friday morning, weak wave looks to slide southeast from SD \ninto IA. Ahead of this wave, 850mb WAA will increase across IA, \nincluding over NE IA and nearby parts WI. Progged soundings suggest \nsome elevated instability will be present as a result. Thus, good \nchance (30-50%) for some showers and thunderstorms to develop there \nFriday morning. With potential (20-40%) for MUCAPE to build to \naround 500 J/kg per 08.00z ECENS, cannot totally rule out a strong \nstorm with a risk for hail. That said, shear through the effective \nlayer of the storm appears to be weak (20-30 kts) and relatively \nwarm temperatures at around 550mb look to limit instability. \nTherefore, am not particularly excited about the prospect for a \nsevere hail report.\n\nWarm and windy Saturday\n\nFriday into Saturday, longwave trough moves over the Canadian \nRockies with a southwesterly upper jet on the equator-ward side of \nthis feature. Strong lee surface cyclone will develop as a result \nand, as this feature shifts eastward into Manitoba, 35-45 kt 850mb \njet looks to develop from E NE to N WI, including over SE MN. The \ncombination of mostly sunny skies, the strong 850mb winds for \nefficient mixing processes to tap into, and an increasing surface \npressure gradient will lead to a breezy to windy day across the CWA, \nparticularly in portions of southeast Minnesota west of the \nsheltered Mississippi valley. 08.13z NBM 24 hour probability for a \n45 mph gust at the Rochester airport (where training data includes \npeak hourly gust information unlike most other grid points in the \nNBM) is 55%, so a Wind Advisory may be needed down the road. Have \nboosted winds above NBM using other consensus blends as it \nfrequently has winds that are too slow on days with good wind \nsetups. Additionally, the combination of the winds and efficient \nmixing bring the possibility of elevated fire weather conditions but \nthe core of the stronger winds and drier air looks to remain to our \nnorthwest.\n\nLow potential for rain Monday\n\nGFS/EC/CMC in good agreement that the base of a longwave positively \ntilted trough will slide over the CWA Monday. This pattern usually \nsuggests issues with sufficient forcing for ascent and moisture for \nprecip and this looks to be no exception to this. Thus, this period \nwill only have a low (15-30%) for precipitation and no hazardous \nweather is expected.",
